  "summary": "DSSD first swaps SSD's VGG backbone for Residual-101 with an output stride of 16, adds a prediction module (a residual block) before each prediction layer, then adds an asymmetric hourglass deconvolution side branch with feed-forward skip connections and batch normalization, fusing decoder and encoder features by element-wise product before prediction.",
  "problemSummary": "Single-shot detectors put all detection on the deepest ConvNet layer (or on multi-scale maps that add cost), so one layer must cover all object scales and shallow maps lack semantics for small objects; naively combining a deep Residual-101 classifier with SSD, or appending deconvolution layers, does not work out of the box.",
  "contributions": [
   "Augment a Residual-101-based SSD with a deconvolution hourglass that passes large-scale context down through feed-forward connections and element-wise feature combination before each prediction layer.",
   "Residual-101 base network with output stride reduced from 32 to 16 (atrous convolution in conv5)",
   "Prediction module with a residual block before each prediction layer",
   "Asymmetric encoder-decoder hourglass of learned deconvolution layers with skip connections",
   "Batch normalization after each convolution in the deconvolution module",
   "Element-wise product (with element-wise sum as alternative) feature combination",
   "One extra prior-box aspect ratio (1.6) added to (2.0, 3.0)"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "DSSD is not as fast as the original SSD due to Residual-101, extra modules, and more default boxes.",
   "2.6 times more default boxes than SSD slow both prediction and non-maximum suppression.",
   "No pre-trained model exists for the decoder, so its layers start from random initialization.",
   "Adding deconvolution layers improved small-object AP on COCO but gave no improvement on large objects.",
   "Fine-tuning the whole network after the deconvolution module decreased performance."
  ],
