  "summary": "MegDet trains a detector with mini-batches up to 256 on up to 128 GPUs by (a) re-deriving the linear scaling rule from a variance-equivalence assumption and applying a linear warmup schedule, and (b) introducing Cross-GPU Batch Normalization, which aggregates BN mean/variance across GPUs with an AllReduce so BN statistics come from enough images.",
  "problemSummary": "Object detectors train with tiny mini-batches (2-16 images), unlike classification where batches of thousands are routine, which makes training slow, gives BN no accurate per-batch statistics, and yields imbalanced positive/negative samples; simply scaling the batch fails because a large learning rate breaks convergence while a small one loses accuracy.",
  "contributions": [
   "Scale the learning rate linearly with a gradual warmup and compute batch-normalization statistics across all GPUs instead of per device, making large-mini-batch detector training converge quickly and accurately.",
   "linear scaling rule re-derived under a variance equivalence assumption",
   "linear gradual warmup of the learning rate at the start of training",
   "Cross-GPU Batch Normalization (CGBN) computing aggregated mean/variance via AllReduce with NCCL",
   "sub-linear memory to fit larger BN batches (32/64 images) into GPU memory",
   "FPN detection framework on a ResNet-50 backbone",
   "normal and long learning-rate decay policies (epoch-based halvings)"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Without batch normalization, batch-64 and batch-128 trainings fail to converge even with warmup; batch-128 fails with both warmup and half learning rate.",
   "Larger BN statistics batches hurt: raising BN size from 32 to 64 drops accuracy, showing a mismatch between classification and detection.",
   "Cross-GPU BN is restricted to GPUs on the same machine, limiting how many images enter the statistics.",
   "Sub-linear memory used for larger BN batches slightly compromises training speed.",
   "Large-batch epoch-by-epoch accuracy behaves differently from image classification, and the cause is not understood."
  ],
