  "summary": "CornerNet detects each object as a pair of keypoints: an hourglass network predicts top-left and bottom-right corner heatmaps, per-corner embeddings that group corners of the same instance, and offsets that refine corner locations. A new corner pooling layer looks toward object boundaries so corners lying outside the object can be localized.",
  "problemSummary": "One-stage detectors depend on hand-designed anchor boxes: enormous anchor sets are needed to cover boxes densely, only a tiny fraction ever matches ground truth (severe positive/negative imbalance that slows training), and anchor sizes, aspect ratios and matching thresholds add many ad-hoc hyperparameters.",
  "contributions": [
   "Replace anchor boxes by predicting two corner heatmaps grouped via associative embeddings and refined by offsets, with corner pooling extending feature evidence toward object boundaries.",
   "top-left and bottom-right corner heatmaps (C channels, no background channel)",
   "associative embeddings with pull and push losses",
   "corner pooling layer on each prediction branch",
   "corner offset prediction with smooth L1 loss",
   "reduced Gaussian penalty for negative locations near positives (object-dependent radius)",
   "hourglass-104 backbone with two prediction modules and intermediate supervision"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Corner detection is the bottleneck: ground-truth heatmaps alone raise AP from 38.4% to 73.1%.",
   "Grouping errors remain: corners from different objects can receive similar embeddings and be merged.",
   "Small-object AP (19.1 single-scale) is far below large-object AP (54.3) on test-dev.",
   "Average inference time is 244 ms per image (original + flipped fusion), so it is not real time.",
   "Multi-scale CornerNet (42.2 AP) still trails D-RFCN + SNIP (45.7 AP) on test-dev."
  ],
