  "summary": "The paper's method is a controlled experimental recipe: train standard Mask R-CNN (ResNet/ResNeXt + FPN) from random initialization with only two necessary changes - appropriate normalization (GroupNorm or SyncBN, or normalized initialization for VGG) and training schedules long enough (5x/6x) to compensate for the missing pre-training - while keeping architectures and hyper-parameters identical to the fine-tuned counterparts.",
  "problemSummary": "The field assumes detectors must be fine-tuned from ImageNet-pretrained networks and that from-scratch training is non-trivial, while the actual detection gains from ever-larger pre-training sets are small and diminishing. Comparisons are also confounded because the cost of ImageNet pre-training is usually ignored and short schedules hide what from-scratch training really needs.",
  "contributions": [
   "Train unmodified baseline detectors from random initialization with batch-size-independent normalization and 5x-6x longer schedules, then compare against identical ImageNet-pretrained systems under controlled hyper-parameters.",
   "GroupNorm or Synchronized BatchNorm replacing all frozen-BN layers",
   "normalized (MSRA) initialization enabling VGG nets to train without BN/GN",
   "extended training schedules (2x up to 6x, and 9x/11x in enhanced settings)",
   "identical architectures and hyper-parameters for scratch and pre-trained runs",
   "sample-count analysis (images, instances, pixels) to argue fairness of longer training",
   "evaluations across bbox AP, mask AP, keypoint AP, augmentation and cascade variants"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Requires much longer training: 540k iterations (6x schedule), and up to 9x/11x in enhanced or VGG settings.",
   "Breaks down in very small data regimes: with 1k COCO images, scratch reaches only 3.5-5.4 AP vs 9.9 AP with pre-training; the breakdown point lies between 3.5k and 10k COCO images.",
   "On PASCAL VOC, training from scratch gives 77.6 mAP versus 82.7 mAP with ImageNet pre-training and never catches up.",
   "Small batch sizes degrade BatchNorm, so frozen-BN style training from scratch is invalid and normalization must be changed.",
   "Training for longer on the small learning rate stage often leads to overfitting."
  ],
