  "solutionTags": [
   "keypoint-based",
   "two-stage",
   "grid-point-localization"
  ],
  "summary": "Grid R-CNN replaces the offset regression branch with a fully convolutional grid branch: RoIAlign features are refined by dilated convolutions and deconvolutions into 56x56 N-by-N probability heatmaps supervised at grid-point locations, box edges are then computed as probability-weighted averages of the predicted grid points. Two-order feature fusion exchanges information between neighboring grid points' feature maps, and an extended region mapping makes the heatmap cover a twice-larger image region so out-of-proposal grid points are still labeled.",
  "problemSummary": "Detectors localize boxes by regressing offsets through fully connected layers, which collapses the spatial layout of the feature map into a vector and loses explicit spatial information, so localization quality is poor under strict IoU criteria. Point-based localization is also fragile because the corner points of an object often lie outside the object body, where the local features are just background.",
  "contributions": [
   "Predict N-by-N heatmap locations of grid points for each RoI with an FCN branch, fuse neighbor grid-point features in two orders, and take probability-weighted averages of grid points on each box edge as the box coordinates.",
   "grid prediction branch: RoIAlign 14x14, eight 3x3 dilated convs, two 2x deconvolutions, N-by-N heatmaps at 56x56",
   "multi-point supervision with 5 positive pixels in a cross shape and binary cross-entropy loss",
   "probability-weighted aggregation of grid points per box edge",
   "first-order and second-order grid points feature fusion with per-point filter groups",
   "extended region mapping covering a twice larger region than the proposal",
   "inference pipeline: RPN RoIs, NMS at 0.5 IoU, top-125 RoIs into the grid branch, final NMS"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "AP50 is slightly lower than the regression baseline (e.g. 59.3 to 58.3 on FPN ResNet-50), suggesting the grid branch can slightly hurt the classification branch.",
   "Categories with round shapes without structural edges (sports ball, frisbee, bowl, clock, cup) show declines or the least gains.",
   "Techniques such as SNIP-style scale selection and Cascade R-CNN cascading are not applied in the current framework.",
   "Simply enlarging proposals to cover grid points brings no gain and harms small-object accuracy, requiring the extended region mapping workaround."
  ],
