  "summary": "ASSD embeds a small fully differentiable attention unit between SSD's feature maps and prediction heads: it projects features to queries/keys/values, builds an NxN softmax attention map of pixel-level relations, and adds the reweighted features back residually, optionally after fusing semantics from deeper layers into conv3.",
  "problemSummary": "One-stage detectors are fast but less accurate than two-stage detectors, largely because of foreground/background imbalance among dense anchors, while existing fixes such as RetinaNet's focal loss are parameter sensitive and heavier SSD refinements (DSSD/FSSD) cost computation.",
  "contributions": [
   "Use a Transformer-style self-attention unit on each SSD feature map to highlight useful regions and suppress irrelevant ones before box regression and classification, keeping SSD's overall structure.",
   "attention unit with query/key/value projections (C' = C/8) and softmax attention map",
   "residual feature update x_s = x_s + (a_s v(x_s)^T)^T",
   "semantic fusion of conv4 and conv5 into conv3 with bilinear upsampling and 1x1 conv",
   "ResNet101 backbone with SSD-style extra blocks conv6-9",
   "multi-scale predictions from conv3-9",
   "hard negative mining and SSD anchor/loss settings unchanged"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Semantic fusion alone does not notably improve accuracy and adds computational overhead.",
   "The full attention+fusion model runs slower than plain SSD513 (0.1648s vs 0.1417s per image on a K40).",
   "ASSD retains SSD's anchor boxes and hard negative mining, so the dense-anchor design and its imbalance issue are mitigated rather than removed."
  ],
