  "summary": "Deformable DETR replaces the Transformer attention modules that process feature maps with multi-scale deformable attention, which samples a small set of key points around a reference point from multi-scale feature maps, giving linear complexity and 10x faster convergence; iterative box refinement and a two-stage variant further raise accuracy.",
  "problemSummary": "DETR is the first fully end-to-end detector but converges very slowly (500 epochs on COCO) and handles small objects poorly, because Transformer attention over image feature maps is quadratic in pixel count and starts near-uniform.",
  "contributions": [
   "Attend only to a small fixed set of learned sampling locations around a reference point (deformable style) instead of all pixels, aggregated across four feature levels inside a DETR encoder-decoder.",
   "multi-scale deformable attention module (default M = 8 heads, K = 4 sampling points)",
   "deformable Transformer encoder over C3-C6 multi-scale feature maps without FPN",
   "decoder cross-attention replaced by multi-scale deformable attention, self-attention unchanged",
   "object-query reference points predicted via linear projection and sigmoid",
   "box prediction as relative offsets to the reference point",
   "iterative bounding box refinement across decoder layers"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Deformable attention is still slightly slower than traditional convolution because of unordered memory access.",
   "Inference is about 25% slower than Faster R-CNN + FPN despite on-par FLOPs.",
   "Best test-dev results (52.3 AP) require test-time augmentation; base ResNet-50 model reaches 46.9 AP.",
   "Only cross-attention is deformable; decoder self-attention is left unchanged."
  ],
