  "summary": "Sparse R-CNN replaces the RPN with a fixed set of N (default 100) learnable proposal boxes plus learnable proposal features; RoIAlign features are processed by a Dynamic Instance Interactive Head whose 1x1 convolution parameters are generated per proposal, iterated over six stages with self-attention and feature reuse, and trained with a one-to-one set prediction loss, yielding NMS-free end-to-end predictions.",
  "problemSummary": "Mainstream detectors depend on dense candidates (anchors, reference points, or RPN proposals), which forces NMS post-processing, many-to-one label assignment sensitive to heuristics, and heavy sensitivity to candidate design; DETR removes those components but each object query must interact with the whole image feature, so it converges slowly.",
  "contributions": [
   "Move from dense or dense-to-sparse detection to purely sparse detection by learning a small fixed set of proposals and conditioning each detection head on its own proposal feature instead of dense candidates and shared weights.",
   "fixed sparse set of learnable proposal boxes (N x 4) replacing RPN candidates",
   "learnable proposal features (N x 256) encoding instance characteristics",
   "Dynamic Instance Interactive Head whose two 1x1 conv parameters come from the proposal feature",
   "RoIAlign feature extraction for each proposal box",
   "iterative cascade of 6 stages with self-attention and previous-stage feature reuse",
   "set prediction loss with one-to-one bipartite matching (focal, L1 and GIoU terms)"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Naively swapping RPN proposals for learnable boxes collapses AP from 40.2 to 18.5 without the iterative dynamic head.",
   "A simple cascade architecture without feature reuse makes no big difference.",
   "Beyond 6 stages performance is saturated while FPS drops from 23 to 17 and training time doubles.",
   "Increasing proposals from 100 to 500 raises training time from 19h to 60h for small gains."
  ],
