  "summary": "Efficient DETR adds a dense prior to set prediction: a class-specific sliding-window head produces proposals, and the top-K (K=100) scored 4-d proposals with their 256-d encoder features become the reference points and object queries of a 3-encoder, 1-decoder transformer, which refines them with one-to-one Hungarian assignment instead of a 6-layer decoder cascade.",
  "problemSummary": "The paper argues that end-to-end transformer detectors like DETR and Deformable DETR only work well because they stack 6 decoder layers in a cascade, and that the real cause is the random initialization of object containers (object queries and reference points); this makes training slow (10-20x more epochs than mainstream detectors) while dense anchor-based detectors still depend on hand-crafted NMS to be usable.",
  "contributions": [
   "Initialize object queries and reference points from top-K dense sliding-window proposals (dense prior) so that a single decoder layer with one-to-one Hungarian matching suffices, eliminating both the cascade and NMS.",
   "Dense part: class-specific sliding-window prediction generating candidate proposals, sharing the detection head with the sparse part",
   "Top-K (K=100) selection of 4-d proposals and their 256-d features as reference points and object queries",
   "3 encoder layers and only 1 decoder layer, without the 6-layer cascade structure",
   "Dynamically tuned proposal number: starts at 300 and decreases linearly to 100 during training",
   "One-to-one label assignment via the Hungarian algorithm shared by dense and sparse parts"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "0.3 AP below Sparse R-CNN on COCO 2017 val (44.2 vs 44.5) for the 3-encoder, 1-decoder ResNet-50 model.",
   "A large number of proposals may bring more false positives to the final results, especially in crowded scenes.",
   "Cascading decoder layers after Efficient DETR brings only slight improvement and degrades performance after two iterations."
  ],
