  "summary": "PP-YOLOv2 stacks empirically verified refinements on PP-YOLO: a PAN neck with skip connections and Mish applied in the neck (backbone untouched), input size raised to 640 for training and evaluation (up to 768, batch reduced to 12 images per GPU), and a soft-label IoU-aware branch, each added incrementally so mAP rises while inference time stays almost unchanged.",
  "problemSummary": "For practical deployment, YOLOv3-style one-stage detectors must keep inference speed while closing the accuracy gap with two-stage detectors, yet individually promising refinements often fail to combine, so how to reliably improve YOLOv3 without losing speed is unsolved.",
  "contributions": [
   "Combine refinements that measurably help in incremental ablations (PAN + Mish in the neck, larger input size, soft-label IoU-aware loss) on top of PP-YOLO, rejecting tricks that do not transfer to full COCO training.",
   "PAN detection neck with added skip connections, Mish applied in the detection neck instead of the backbone",
   "Larger input size: batch reduced from 24 to 12 images per GPU, largest input expanded from 608 to 768, sampled evenly from [320 ... 768]",
   "IoU-aware branch with a soft-label loss format instead of the previous soft-weight format",
   "PP-YOLO baseline with ResNet50-vd backbone and its existing tricks (Deformable Conv, SSLD, CoordConv, DropBlock, SPP)"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Infer time and FPS only consider the FP32 model and exclude result decoding and NMS.",
   "Cosine learning rate decay is hyper-parameter sensitive and showed no positive effect on COCO train2017.",
   "Backbone parameter freezing gains 1 mAP on COCO minitrain but decreases mAP by 0.8% on COCO train2017.",
   "Replacing Mish with SiLU drops 0.5% mAP on COCO train2017, and the reason is unknown to the authors.",
   "Some tricks behave inconsistently between COCO minitrain and COCO train2017, raising doubts about minitrain-based conclusions."
  ],
