  "summary": "QueryInst attaches six dynamic mask heads (dynamic mask convolution plus a vanilla mask head) in parallel to a Sparse R-CNN query detector and trains them simultaneously, so mask gradients are written into object queries that are in one-to-one correspondence across stages; only the final stage is kept at inference.",
  "problemSummary": "Query-based detectors match detection SOTA, but instance segmentation in query frameworks is unsolved: grafting Cascade Mask R-CNN/HTC multi-stage mask practices onto query detectors is inefficient and underperforms, because few queries limit training samples and explicit mask-head connections slow training and inference.",
  "contributions": [
   "Exploit the intrinsic one-to-one correspondence of object queries to carry per-mask information across stages via parallel-supervised dynamic mask heads instead of explicit multi-stage mask connections.",
   "Sparse R-CNN as the multi-stage query based object detector with six query stages",
   "Dynamic mask head at each stage: dynamic mask convolution DynConvmask_t followed by a vanilla mask head M_t",
   "Parallel supervision on all mask heads so mask gradients also update the object queries",
   "Shared query and multi-head self-attention between the detection and instance segmentation sub-networks",
   "Inference keeps only final-stage predictions, discarding intermediate dynamic mask heads"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "The number of queries in the framework is much smaller than the number of proposals in Cascade Mask R-CNN and HTC, limiting training samples.",
   "Without dynamic mask convolution the mask heads in different stages are isolated and QueryInst degenerates to Cascade Mask R-CNN with a fixed number of proposals.",
   "With modest data augmentation the 3x schedule is already near optimum, and performance begins to degenerate under longer training schedules.",
   "Explicit mask feature flow as in HTC is redundant and harmful for model efficiency, so that route to accuracy is closed off."
  ],
