  "summary": "YOLOX upgrades the YOLOv3/YOLOv5 pipelines with a lite decoupled head, an anchor-free single prediction per location, center-based multi-positive sampling, the SimOTA dynamic top-k label assignment, and Mosaic+MixUp strong augmentation, trained from scratch; an optional NMS-free end-to-end module is also studied.",
  "problemSummary": "YOLOv4 and YOLOv5 are still anchor-based detectors with hand-crafted assigning rules, so recent advances (anchor-free detection, advanced label assignment, NMS-free end-to-end detection) have not reached the YOLO family; anchors themselves need domain-specific clustering, complicate the head, and inflate the prediction traffic that can bottleneck edge devices.",
  "contributions": [
   "Make YOLO anchor-free with one direct box prediction per location, decouple classification/regression heads, and assign positives globally with the simplified SimOTA strategy.",
   "lite decoupled head: 1x1 conv to 256 channels, then two parallel 3x3 branches for classification and regression plus an IoU branch",
   "anchor-free prediction: one prediction per location directly regressing left-top offsets and box height/width",
   "multi positives: center 3x3 area sampling of positives (center sampling as in FCOS)",
   "SimOTA label assignment: pairwise classification+regression cost, center prior, dynamic top-k positives, global view"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "The optional end-to-end (NMS-free) configuration lowers AP and slows inference, so it is not part of the released final models.",
   "Larger models such as Scaled-YOLOv4/YOLOv5-P6 and transformer-based detectors pushing ~60 AP were not explored in this report.",
   "Speed numbers in the SOTA comparison table are not controlled, as they vary with software and hardware."
  ],
