  "summary": "DAB-DETR formulates queries as dynamic 4D anchor boxes (x, y, w, h): the box coordinates are positionally encoded into positional queries, updated layer-by-layer by a shared prediction head, and the box width and height modulate the Gaussian positional attention map (plus a tunable temperature), so cross-attention behaves like a cascade of soft ROI pooling.",
  "problemSummary": "DETR's learnable queries are an ineffective design that causes extremely slow training convergence (about 500 epochs) and produce multi-mode, nearly uniform positional attention maps that cannot localize objects reliably. Prior DETR follow-ups only associate queries with 2D positions and assume a fixed-size isotropic positional prior, ignoring object scale.",
  "contributions": [
   "Directly learn 4D anchor boxes as decoder queries, refine them layer-by-layer, and use their width and height to modulate the positional cross-attention.",
   "anchor boxes (x, y, w, h) as positional queries via shared MLP over sinusoidal encodings",
   "layer-by-layer anchor update from predicted relative offsets",
   "width and height modulated Gaussian positional attention",
   "temperature tuning of positional encoding (T = 20)",
   "conditional spatial query scaling inherited from Conditional DETR",
   "300 anchor queries with 3 pattern embeddings"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "The model has trouble with dense objects, very small objects, and very large objects.",
   "The design is single-scale: no feature pyramid or multi-scale technique is used.",
   "Reported GFLOPs show a slight increase over Conditional DETR (per the authors' comparison of published numbers).",
   "In DAB-Deformable-DETR, total loss over all decoder layers stays higher than Deformable DETR even though the final-layer loss is lower."
  ],
