  "summary": "SAHI provides a detector-agnostic pipeline: during slicing aided fine-tuning the training set is augmented with overlapping image patches resized larger so relative object sizes grow, and during slicing aided hyper inference the query image is cut into overlapping patches that are each run through the detector (optionally together with full-image inference), with predictions merged back into original coordinates via NMS.",
  "problemSummary": "Small objects and objects far away in the scene are a major challenge in surveillance and aerial imaging: they occupy few pixels, lack detail, and cause detectors trained on standard low-resolution datasets with large objects to lose accuracy on high-resolution drone and surveillance imagery, while high-resolution inputs also raise memory requirements.",
  "contributions": [
   "Slice high-resolution images into overlapping patches, run the detector per patch (and optionally on the full image), then merge the predictions with NMS in original coordinates.",
   "overlapping M x N patch slicing with hyper-parameter size ranges",
   "aspect-ratio-preserving patch resize (width 800-1333 pixels)",
   "per-patch detector forward pass",
   "optional full-inference (FI) on the original image for large objects",
   "NMS merging with match threshold Tm and detection threshold Td",
   "slicing aided fine-tuning (SF) dataset augmentation with patches plus original images"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "As patch sizes decrease, larger objects may not fit within a slice, harming detection of large objects.",
   "Sliced inference increases computational time linearly (though memory stays fixed).",
   "Error analysis shows major remaining room on false positives and false negatives.",
   "Adding full-image inference raises large-object AP but slightly decreases small/medium AP.",
   "Evaluation is limited to AP50 at a single IoU threshold of 0.5."
  ],
  "datasets": [
   "VisDrone",
   "xView",
   "COCO"
  ],
