  "summary": "DN-DETR adds an auxiliary denoising task: noised ground-truth boxes and labels are fed to the Transformer decoder as extra queries and trained to reconstruct the originals, which bypasses the unstable bipartite matching; an attention mask prevents information leakage between the denoising and matching parts.",
  "problemSummary": "DETR-like detectors train very slowly because their one-to-one supervision relies on bipartite (Hungarian) matching, which is unstable early in training and gives decoder queries inconsistent optimization goals from epoch to epoch.",
  "contributions": [
   "Feed noised GT box-label pairs as extra decoder queries and reconstruct them with a matching-free reconstruction loss added to the standard Hungarian loss.",
   "noised GT box queries using center shifting and box scaling noise",
   "label flipping with class label embeddings plus an indicator to support label denoising",
   "reconstruction loss (l1 + GIoU for boxes, focal loss for labels) alongside the Hungarian loss",
   "attention mask blocking leakage from the denoising part to the matching part and among denoising groups",
   "multiple noised versions (denoising groups) of each GT object per image",
   "denoising part removed at inference, so there is no testing overhead"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Noise is sampled only from a uniform distribution; more complex noising schemes were not explored.",
   "Accuracy drops when the noise scale is too large.",
   "Denoising raises per-epoch training time (57.7 min vs 51.1 min for the DAB-DETR-R50 baseline).",
   "Without an attention mask, denoising training degrades performance instead of improving it.",
   "Benefits for segmentation and traditional CNN detectors are shown only in preliminary adaptations."
  ],
