  "summary": "DiffusionDet formulates detection as denoising diffusion over box coordinates: training pads and noises ground-truth boxes and learns to reverse the process with a set prediction loss, while inference starts from random Gaussian boxes and progressively refines them with DDIM steps and box renewal; a single-run image encoder feeds a 6-stage Sparse R-CNN-style decoder that is reused across steps, giving dynamic box counts and iterative evaluation with shared parameters.",
  "problemSummary": "Modern detectors regress and classify on hand-designed object candidates (sliding windows, proposals, anchors, reference points), and even DETR-style models depend on a fixed set of learnable queries; no prior method lets detection start from arbitrary random boxes, vary the number of evaluation boxes, or reuse the detection head iteratively across sparse and crowded scenes.",
  "contributions": [
   "Cast object detection as a generative denoising process from noisy boxes to object boxes, refined iteratively at inference by a reusable detection decoder conditioned on once-computed image features.",
   "forward diffusion corrupting padded ground-truth boxes with scheduled Gaussian noise",
   "image encoder (ResNet or Swin with FPN) executed once per image",
   "6-stage detection decoder cropping RoI features from the feature map",
   "set prediction loss with optimal-transport top-k assignment",
   "DDIM sampling to step boxes between timesteps at inference",
   "box renewal replacing low-scoring predictions with fresh random boxes"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Inference requires iterative denoising sampling, which can be slower than single-pass detectors.",
   "Only the basic DDIM diffusion strategy is used, without more advanced fast samplers."
  ],
