  "summary": "MS-DETR mixes one-to-one supervision with additional one-to-many supervision applied directly to the object queries of the primary (inference) decoder, using an extra box/class prediction head that is active only during training so inference is unchanged.",
  "problemSummary": "DETR is trained with one-to-one supervision that promotes a single candidate per ground-truth object but gives no direct supervision to the candidate generation step, which slows training and yields lower-quality detection candidates than NMS-based detectors at the same budget.",
  "contributions": [
   "Add a one-to-many prediction head (box + class predictors) supervised with top-K matching on the primary decoder queries, optionally after reordering decoder layers to cross-attention then self-attention, with no extra decoders or queries.",
   "one-to-many box and class predictors on the primary decoder",
   "matching score combining classification score and IoU with weight alpha and threshold tau",
   "decoder layer order cross-attention -> self-attention -> FFN",
   "one-to-many supervision placed on internal queries output from cross-attention",
   "shared weights between one-to-one and one-to-many predictors"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Gains over the strongest baseline decrease with longer training schedules (+1.1 mAP at 24 epochs vs +2.4 at 12 epochs over DINO).",
   "One-to-many matching depends on tuned hyperparameters K, threshold tau and score weight alpha.",
   "Experiments are restricted to DETR-family detectors with ResNet-50 backbones on COCO.",
   "Placing the one-to-many supervision on the wrong decoder signals (internal self-attention queries) degrades results."
  ],
