  "summary": "Proposes Programmable Gradient Information (PGI), an auxiliary supervision framework with a main inference branch, an auxiliary reversible branch that supplies reliable gradients, and multi-level auxiliary information that aggregates gradients from all prediction heads; together with GELAN, a generalized ELAN/CSPNet architecture that supports arbitrary computational blocks, combined into YOLOv9.",
  "problemSummary": "Deep networks lose a large amount of input information as data passes layer by layer (information bottleneck), producing biased/unreliable gradients and poor convergence; existing remedies (reversible architectures, masked modeling, deep supervision) each have drawbacks and deep supervision in particular only works for very deep models and degrades lightweight ones.",
  "contributions": [
   "Generate reliable gradients through an auxiliary reversible branch (used only during training) plus multi-level auxiliary information, and plan gradient paths with the GELAN backbone so both lightweight and deep models learn complete information.",
   "main branch (used at inference only)",
   "auxiliary reversible branch (e.g., ICN with DHLC linkage)",
   "multi-level auxiliary information integrating gradients from different prediction heads",
   "GELAN (CSP-ELAN) block aggregating ELAN with CSPNet",
   "lead-head guided assignment from YOLOv7 applied to PGI's auxiliary supervision",
   "anchor-free decoupled prediction head"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "GELAN without PGI shows divergence when detecting object boundaries and spurious background responses.",
   "Conventional deep supervision (PFH) is only effective in deep models and hurts shallow/general models.",
   "Evaluation limited to MS COCO 2017 with a single 500-epoch training recipe; no inference latency (FPS) measurements are reported in the main comparison tables.",
   "Training requires extra auxiliary branches (reversible branch and multi-level heads), even though inference is unaffected."
  ],
