  "summary": "The paper presents consistent dual assignments for NMS-free training (an extra one-to-one head jointly trained with the one-to-many head under a consistent matching metric, keeping only the one-to-one head at inference) plus a holistic efficiency-accuracy driven model design (lightweight classification head, spatial-channel decoupled downsampling, rank-guided CIB blocks, large-kernel convolution and partial self-attention), yielding the YOLOv10 family.",
  "problemSummary": "YOLOs depend on NMS post-processing, which blocks true end-to-end deployment and hurts inference latency, while the many components of YOLO architectures have never been comprehensively inspected, leaving noticeable computational redundancy and suboptimal efficiency-accuracy trade-offs.",
  "contributions": [
   "Eliminate NMS via dual one-to-many/one-to-one label assignments with a consistent matching metric, and holistically redesign YOLO components for efficiency and accuracy.",
   "consistent dual label assignments (one-to-many + one-to-one heads)",
   "consistent matching metric shared by both heads",
   "lightweight classification head with depthwise separable convolutions",
   "spatial-channel decoupled downsampling",
   "rank-guided block design with the compact inverted block (CIB)",
   "large-kernel depthwise convolution in deep stages"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "No pre-training on large-scale datasets such as Objects365 due to limited computational resources.",
   "Performance gap between NMS-free and one-to-many+NMS training remains for small models (1.0 AP on YOLOv10-N, 0.5 AP on YOLOv10-S).",
   "Large-kernel convolution brings no improvement for the medium scale (YOLOv10-M) due to its inherently extensive receptive field."
  ],
