  "summary": "DEIMv2 extends DEIM with DINOv3 features across eight model sizes: a Spatial Tuning Adapter converts DINOv3's single-scale output into multi-scale features and enhances them with a lightweight detail branch, while a simplified decoder (SwiGLU FFN, RMSNorm, shared query position embedding) and object-level CopyBlend augmentation strengthen Dense O2O; ultra-light variants instead use progressively pruned HGNetv2-B0 backbones.",
  "problemSummary": "Real-time detectors must balance accuracy against compute for edge and mobile use, and it was unclear how to fold DINOv3's strong foundation features into DETR-style real-time models: DINOv3 only emits single-scale features while detection needs multi-scale ones, and deployment scales from GPU down to mobile must all be covered.",
  "contributions": [
   "Combine DINOv3-pretrained/distilled ViT backbones with a parameter-free Spatial Tuning Adapter for large variants, and pruned HGNetv2 CNN backbones for ultra-light variants, all on an RT-DETR-style architecture with a simplified decoder and upgraded Dense O2O matching.",
   "Spatial Tuning Adapter (STA): fully convolutional network that resizes 1/16-scale features from ViT blocks (e.g. 5th, 8th, 11th) into multiple scales via parameter-free bilinear interpolation and adds a Bi-Fusion ultra-lightweight CNN detail branch",
   "DINOv3-pretrained ViT-Small / ViT-Small+ backbones for X and L, ViT-Tiny / ViT-Tiny+ distilled from DINOv3 for M and S",
   "Progressively depth- and width-pruned HGNetv2-B0 backbones for Nano, Pico, Femto and Atto",
   "Simplified decoder: SwiGLU FFN and RMSNorm replacing FFN and LayerNorm, plus one position embedding shared across all decoder layers",
   "Enhanced Dense O2O with object-level CopyBlend augmentation that blends new objects into images",
   "Five-term weighted loss (MAL, FGL, DDF, L1 bbox, GIoU), with the local FGL/DDF losses excluded for Pico, Femto and Atto"
  ],
  "limitations": [
   "Latency of the proposed models has not been optimized; techniques such as Flash Attention could further accelerate inference.",
   "Accuracy gains over DEIM come mostly from medium and large objects; small-object detection remains a challenge across scales.",
   "FGL and DDF local losses degrade performance of ultra-lightweight models, so they are excluded from training Pico, Femto and Atto."
  ],
